**(i)** Let $v\in V$ and let $k\ge0$ be minimal with $T^kv=0$ (it exists since $T$ is nilpotent). Suppose $k\ge2$ and put $w=T^{k-1}v$. Then $w\ne0$ and $Tw=T^kv=0$, so $w\in N$. For every $n\in N$,
$$(w,n)=(T(T^{k-2}v),n)=(T^{k-2}v,Tn)=(T^{k-2}v,0)=0 .$$
So $w\in N$ and $(w,N)=0$; nondegeneracy on $N$ gives $w=0$, a contradiction. Hence $k\le1$, i.e. $Tv=0$. As $v$ was arbitrary, $T=0$.

**(ii)** Let $V=K^2$ with basis $e_1,e_2$, let $Te_1=e_2$, $Te_2=0$ (so $T\ne0$, $T^2=0$), and let the form have Gram matrix $\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$, i.e. $(e_1,e_1)=(e_2,e_2)=0$, $(e_1,e_2)=(e_2,e_1)=1$. Its determinant is $-1\neq0$, so the form is nondegenerate on $V$. By bilinearity it suffices to check $(Tx,y)=(x,Ty)$ on basis vectors:
- $x=y=e_1$: $(e_2,e_1)=1=(e_1,e_2)$;
- $x=e_1,y=e_2$: $(e_2,e_2)=0=(e_1,0)$;
- $x=e_2,y=e_1$: $(0,e_1)=0=(e_2,e_2)$;
- $x=y=e_2$: both sides $0$.

(Here $N=Ke_2$ and $(e_2,e_2)=0$, so the form is degenerate on $N$, consistent with (i).) ∎
